   From: "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>

   I'm confused.  Are you using "nation" as a particular term of art?  I've
   never understood nation to mean "composed of a single race".  I've always
   thought that the U.S. is a nation-state but clearly "American" is not a
   particular race.  Though on further reflection, there are clearly some
   problems with my understanding.  First, nation-state would seem to be
   redundant, yet it clearly refers to something other than just nation or
   just state.  Second, how can a "nation-state" be composed of individual
   "states"? 

I believe that the term "nation state" was applied to the states that
arose in the late middle ages in Europe.  The concept is that not only
is it a State, but also that it has a sense of "nationhood", some sort
of ethnic solidarity, involving a common language, history-mythology,
culture, etc.  Thus, for a long while the Germans were a nation but
not a state.  The French are very much a nation-state and have been
for centuries.

Of course, much of this is backwards -- the concept of nationality is
largely an invention of the governments of the nation-states to
increase the cohesion of their states.  But since people believe it,
it is nonetheless real and a powerful influence on history --
nation-states are much less prone to breaking into pieces than states
that encompass people who consider themselves to be of differing
nationalities (e.g., Yugoslavia, USSR).

"American" is rather strange as a nationality, since "Americans" are
(at present) divided into multiple ethnic categories.  But there is
still a strong sense of American-ness that is easily brought out by
the presence of people of other nations with very different cultures.

