   X-Name: Scott C DeLancey 

   > > As far as I can tell, it's LC to use force to keep someone from
   > > trespassing on my land, [etc.]
   > 
   > Fallacy alert.  The "context" is that they own their land and you own
   > yours.  [etc.]

   Well on this you're wrong.  You can have seagulls, and domestic
   dogs are weird because they are both bred and socialized for specific
   human purposes.  But among wild social canines, social primates, and
   humans in hunter-gatherer societies, there is no such thing as individual
   land ownership, even in the ethological sense of defended territory.  [etc.]

I think it's more complicated than that.  Various species have
different territorial behaviors, and in some species the territorial
behavior varies from area to area.  Some species have individual male
and female territory systems; birds whose territories overlap mate.
Some species have no individual territories, but the species live in
groups which do.  Some species have no territories at all.  Some
species live in family-like groups which have territories.

The interesting difference between formal property systems and
territories in other species is that in order to maintain posession of
a territory, one has to occupy it.  (To some extent, legal systems
also have provisions for garbage-collecting "abandoned" property.)

As far as I know, the commonest territorial feature is the one that is
*not* replicated in formal property systems -- "personal space".

Dale

