   acculturated people, they tended to be quite hostile--they are aware
   of the idea of land ownership, and find it contemptible).

There seems to be a pattern that, in a culture, a category of thing is
property, can be "owned", only if introducing the structures of
ownership gains the culture something.  (Usually, solving some sort of
tragedy of the commons.)  Among nomadic groups, owning land would be
of no use, so it isn't ownable.  Among farming groups, owning land is
crucial.  (Among information-based groups, owning information is
crucial!)

In the early part of this century, the concept that electro-magnetic
spectrum space can be owned was invented, because we were running out
of it.

Also, the historical theory that one owns the atmosphere infinitely
high was found to be counter-productive (because atmosphere is used in
fundamentally different ways than land), so all the atmosphere
sufficiently high off the ground was nationalized, and subjected to a
different allocation system.

