From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Experiments

I've read the rest of the article on experimental economics.  They
note that the experiments duplicate the results of economic theory
quite well.

One point that was rather interesting is:  When you ask people, they
seem to place a much greater value on gaining new income than avoiding
expenses they are already paying, despite that this is not
economically rational.  However, economic experiments show that their
actual behavior (in a market) weights both equally.  So a market can
correct for delusions, *even in cases where everybody shares the same
delusion.*

On the other hand, in experiments one will often get the equivalent of
"stock market bubbles", even in cases where the participants *have
complete knowledge of the net present value* of the stocks they are
bidding for.

