_________________________________________________________________

> >From: mdh@cl.cam.ac.uk (Mark Hayter)

> >Subject: Re: ATM as a "general purpose" interconnect

> >Keywords: ATM, DAN

> >Organization: U of Cambridge Computer Lab, UK

> In article <coyne.730074885@convex.convex.com> coyne@convex.com
> (Coyne Gibson) writes:

> >A number of peripheral vendors, and interconnect folks (like Ancor)
> >are proposing peripheral-to-host connection models that use FCS
> >as the base technology.  I've yet to hear of anyone in the ATM/cell-relay
> >world propose a similar technology model...
> 
> >An interesting thing to ponder would be the use of ATM as a "unification"
> >technology; employed as host-to-LAN/WAN, host-to-host (i.e. suitability
> >for use as a parallel computing "backplane" or interconnect), and
> >peripheral-to-host (i.e. disk arrays, tape subsystems, etc.) connection.
> 
> The Desk Area Network is proposed in the Oct 1991 issue of
> Operating Syatems Review (Vol 25 No 4), also available as
> Cambridge University Tech report 228. The idea of the DAN is
> to use an ATM interconnect within a machine.
> 
> We are currently building a multimedia workstation around a DAN.
> In the first instance we are using the ATM crossbar switch
> developed in the Fairisle Local Area ATM project, giving us up
> to 16 devices on the DAN.
> 
> The first peripheral, the "ATM camera", was built last year. This
> captures video directly into a stream of ATM cells. The best 
> aproach would be to deal with data directly from the CCD, but we have
> taken the easier option of using a standard camera and digitising.
> The card therefore consists of the digitizing section, a dual port
> buffer sufficient to store 2 scan lines (one arriving, one going to the
> network) and two xilinx 3042 FPGAs.
> 
> A framestore node and an audio/DSP node are currently being constructed.
> The framestore can take directly video streams and (based on the VCI)
> place them in the apropriate window, it also has a command interface
> which will take X (or similar) requests. 
> 
> We are also investigating the use of a DAN as a processor to memory
> interconnect. We have a CPU node which contains a large cache and
> a cache controller which will send a cell across the DAN when a miss
> occurs. This triggers the memory server to return the line. Again we
> are using xilinx devices to implement the controller.
> 
> In article <23501@venera.isi.edu>, finn@isi.edu (Greg Finn) writes:
> 
> |> 	The key issues here are amount of silicon and speed of the
> |> network.  If a "workstation" is going to have several network
> |> interfaces, they had better be very inexpensive.  If the network is
> |> going to replace the backplane function, it must be able to carry data
> |> at or near to DMA transfer rates.
> |> 
> |> 	ATM is far away from those goals at this time.  OC12 speed is
> |> required and OC12 interfaces are very expensive.  For that matter, OC3
> |> rate interfaces are still quite expensive.  ATM may reach these goals
> |> in time.  However, in my personal view it is much too complex to make
> |> that likely in the near term.
> |> 
> |> 	Ideally, one wants the network interface to occupy a very
> |> small amount of silicon so that it could be contained within your
> |> microprocessor chip, display controller chip, disk controller chip,
> |> and so on.  For interfaces within a chassis, this reduces the cost of
> |> the network interface primarily to a question of how many pins one
> |> would want to devote to it, that is, to the added packaging cost.
> |> 
> 
> In our prototype we are speed limited by using xilinx to a 20MHz clock.
> However *for the DAN* devices the network interface takes only a small
> section of one of the control xilinx devices plus possibly a small area
> of memory to store data (64 bytes - 4k). Within a machine there is no
> need for the full line driving system - though you clearly need it
> for the DAN-LAN connection. It is clear from the SCI NodeChip(tm),
> recently anounced by Dolphin SCI Technology, that use of custom
> design would enable an ATM interconnect to be made that could go
> at much higher rates.
> 
>  The advantage of using ATM within the workstation (at least if you
> believe in it for the wide and local areas) is that you genuinely can
> get an ATM cell stream all the way to the end points. Your QoS is not
> clobbered by contention for the bus/dma engine/reformatter. The cell
> payload is carried untouched from source to destination. The destination
> then knows how to unpack the data into the form it requires, for example
> the framestore will know how to unpack (and possibly uncompress) a block
> of video into its framebuffer. The interface at this point can therefore
> be specific and thus simpler.
> 
> |> 	Such networking technologies do exist and have grown out of
> |> the multicomputing arena rather than the networking standards arena.
> |> In a Caltech Mosaic multicomputer chip an 8-channel * 500 Mb/s router
> |> + two 500 Mb/s DMA copy engines occupy 7.6 square millimeters in 5
> |> volt 1.2 micron CMOS.  This is about 10% of the entire microprocessor
> |> chip's active silicon area.  Moving to 3.3 volt 0.8 micron or smaller
> |> CMOS technology in the near future will drop that to about 3.5 square
> |> millimeters and boost the channel speed substantially.
> |> 
> |> 	So the hardware to accomplish what you are suggesting already
> |> exists.  The operating systems and protocols that could deal with such
> |> a bounty of bandwidth and speed are lagging very far behind what
> |> hardware can provide cheaply.  Hanging those multi-channel 500 Mb/s
> |> network interfaces onto today's workstation architectures and
> |> operating systems is a bad joke that I am party to.
> |> 
> |> --- ggf
> |> --
> |> Gregory Finn	(310) 822-1511
> |> Information Sciences Institute, Marina Del Rey, CA 90292
> 
