Date: Sat, 20 Feb 93 13:21:13 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW&MISC: Justification & Land Property Rights

Tim Starr, continuing our discussion of smokers' rights, posts:

>You've chosen a confusing example by placing the actors within a private
>institution with a certain rule.  The paradigm case would be one in which
>you're on your property, she's on hers.

I think this is an example of a thread gone stale.  I orginally brought up
the restaurant example because I believe that smokers' rights are most
often questioned in such contexts.  Tim's "paradigm case" is incredibly
rare.  How often do people exhale smoke across property lines?  I have no
great disagreement with Tim's treatment of torts in such unusual
circumstances, but I don't think it helps us much with the real world.


>If one remains silent about a past
>instance of statism when the subject is brought up then, all else being equal,
>one is implying consent to it. 

I disagree.  I don't think it makes any sense to say "I don't consent to
the Holocaust."  This degrades the notion of consent--it is more powerful
than mere dislike.

>Suppose the case was
>that of the Holocaust or Stalin's liquidation of the "kulaks".  Can we
>consent to these?  Silence equals death.

No we cannot consent to these.  Nor can we *not* consent to them.  But we
can and should criticize them.  "Silence equals death" makes for catchy
rhetoric here, but poor argumentation.  As I have stressed before, I am not
saying that we should accept statism.  I salute your resistance, but
question your use of "consent."  Consent does not mean the same thing as
"judge immoral."  

>There is no such thing as "mere" or "pure" theory apart from the disposition
>of person and property.  A person has to excercise their self-ownership to
>direct their labor to formulate theories, to argue for claims, and to dispose
>themselves in support or opposition of ideas.

The argument appears to be:  Because theory arises from and is shaped by
practice, the two are inseparable.  But that merely confounds causation
with identity.  If practice and theory really were identical, then we could
not make any sense out of your distinct uses of the terms.  

>If theory can't affect persons and property, then why should it matter that
>anti-gay and anti-lesbian theory get promoted?  

I never said theory cannot have effects.  I merely said that cannot consent
to a theory, but rather only to its application.  Once more:  we can and
should evaluate theories, but not by saying we do or don't consent to them.
 We should save consent for situations where it really matters.

I'm ready to stop discussing this, Tim.  It looks as if we agree in
substance, but not in terminology.  I encourage you to reconsider your use
of "consent."  I believe that if we want to give that term the full force
we both want it to convey, we must restrict its use to contexts where it
actually determines social relations--that is, when theory is applied
rather than merely discussed.


